WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.202
-Our next guest has worked
in politics and public service

00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:05.538
as an aide and advisor
to Hillary Rodham Clinton

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.208
during her two presidential
campaigns, as well as her time

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.576
as First Lady, Senator,
and Secretary of State.

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:14.580
Her Memoir "Both/And: A Life in
Many Worlds" is on sale now.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.749
Please welcome to the show
Huma Abedin!

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.584
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.584 --> 00:00:26.659 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:34.767 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:35.868
-Welcome to the show.

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.837
-Thank you for having me,
I'm so excited.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:39.839
-You've been here before
in the green room

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.674
when Hillary has been a guest
on the show.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:45.678
And now you are on camera,
you are on -- in studio.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.447
You've been doing a lot of press
for your book tour.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:49.882
How are you adjusting to being
the focus of attention?

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:52.685
-I still walk backstage

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:55.354
and then keep looking behind me
for Hillary.

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:58.358
Because assuming that's who
you're expecting to talk to,

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:01.427
and then realize I have to go
out and say words.

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:03.930
And I wrote many words,
and now I'm saying many words,

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.765
and I'm having a great time.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:07.433
-I've got to be honest,
you're a natural.

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.102
You're a natural with the words.

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.771
-Seth, we have been here I think
at least five times.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.439
-I think that's right.
-Me with Hillary.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:16.275
And I'm usually a little bit
more productive.

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:18.877
Because usually when you guys
are out here,

00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:21.014
I'm back there ordering
Seamless,

00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:22.816
figuring out what I'm going
to have for dinner,

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:24.284
getting on a conference call.
-Sure.

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:26.118
-So I actually have
a conference call in my ear

00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:27.453
on one side and talking to you.

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:28.854
-While we're having this
conversation.

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:31.291
I bet you're probably pretty
good at that sorta multitasking.

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:33.793
-Multitasking. There's a lot of
multitasking in this book.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:35.794
-So I have to ask --

00:01:35.794 --> 00:01:39.131
you know, this book is about
how you grew up,

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:41.468
and it's about your time
with Hillary.

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:43.602
It's also about
your personal life,

00:01:43.602 --> 00:01:45.771
which was incredibly traumatic.

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:49.108
And, you know, it's very raw.
The details of this book.

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:51.778
Was it traumatic reliving it?

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.079
Or was it cathartic
putting it down on paper?

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:55.949
-You know, this ended up
being --

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.449
I hadn't thought about it
in the moment,

00:01:57.449 --> 00:02:00.385
but it did end up being
a COVID pandemic book.

00:02:00.385 --> 00:02:02.555
I really -- it forced me,

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:05.458
being in one place and space,
sitting and writing.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.527
And I thought it would be
really hard,

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:11.030
but I basically just poured
my heart into the story.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.199
And I found it to be
incredible therapy.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:15.101
I loved the writing process.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.436
I rediscovered what it was like
to write --

00:02:17.436 --> 00:02:19.939
my father always thought I would
be a writer when I grew up.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:23.076
He always told me
the greatest power I held

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:25.912
was in the power of my pen,
if I used it wisely.

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.447
And so I loved it.

00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:31.250
And I feel it as I'm out in the
world, the sense of liberation.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.920
-Also, you know, you have
this very public marriage

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.755
to someone, Anthony Weiner.

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:38.591
And you know,
we all sort of in real time

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.392
are experiencing
what's happening.

00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:43.730
Has it sort of, writing this
book, brought down the walls

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.865
in a way that people now feel
that they can approach you,

00:02:46.865 --> 00:02:48.267
even people that were maybe
close to you

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:49.969
that didn't want to have
these conversations

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.770
or didn't know if you were
willing to have them.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.572
Have you been able to talk
to people more about it,

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:56.075
having written the book?
-You know, Seth, in 2011

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:58.745
when the story first broke
about Anthony,

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.947
and I was not even
12 weeks pregnant,

00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:02.615
I'm working at
the State Department --

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.750
it was the first time
I realized -- I mean, you know,

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.085
you and I started
this conversation,

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.754
that I'm used to being
behind the scenes,

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:10.422
used to being the person not
talked about, being invisible,

00:03:10.422 --> 00:03:12.091
and I really liked that.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:15.127
And that very first trip I took,
I was at an airport,

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:17.796
and a woman comes over and she
puts her arm on my arm

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:19.999
in the bathroom while
I'm washing my hands and says,

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.668
"I know what you're going
through, hang in there."

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:26.405
And that was the first of
what I would say is hundreds,

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:28.474
thousands, maybe, of moments
in the last decade

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:29.975
where people have approached me
on the street.

00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:32.811
I've been at dinner parties, at
restaurants, and somebody will

00:03:32.811 --> 00:03:35.981
come over and say, "Can I talk
to you for a second?"

00:03:35.981 --> 00:03:38.584
And we step outside
and it's almost like

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:41.420
these little group
mini therapy sessions.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:45.557
And if that's a service that
I can -- if that's one thing...

00:03:45.557 --> 00:03:47.793
I try to believe that
there's a silver lining

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:49.462
to everything that happens
in our lives.

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:52.465
And so if I can share,
I'm happy to do it.

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:54.734
And it's been -- it's been
a wonderful connection

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.335
with strangers.
-I'm so glad that you are

00:03:56.335 --> 00:03:58.972
getting that from the experience
of writing the book.

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:02.775
You talk about giving back and
hoping that this, you know,

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.510
can provide a service to people.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.679
There's some very funny things
that you've had to do

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.548
over the course of your career
working for Hillary Clinton.

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:11.250
The one that
I would not have predicted

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.021
is having to fish a dress
out of the East River.

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:16.855
-You know, so I have
a lot of friends

00:04:16.855 --> 00:04:18.524
who now work
in the Biden administration.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:20.526
And the young ones would call me
and say,

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:23.696
"How do I train, what do I do,
and how did you know...?"

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.197
Because I was a 21-year-old

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:28.134
when I walked into the Clinton
White House in 1996.

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:29.435
I had no idea.

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.104
I mean, it was a total
sink-or-swim attitude there.

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:34.540
And I think in part, that was
part of the training.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.375
You know, it was,
let's just see how she does.

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:37.877
And the very first trip

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.845
I took with the President
and First Lady

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:43.049
to New York City on Marine One,
I did not know

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:44.083
that when a president traveled

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.751
there's more than one
helicopter.

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:48.554
I'm on the staff helicopter
with all the luggage.

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:51.056
The helicopter lands.
I take all the luggage off.

00:04:51.056 --> 00:04:54.393
My helicopter takes off
as Marine One is landing.

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:57.730
And the prop wash lifts the
First Lady's clothes,

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.933
the hanging bag,
off the landing zone

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:03.535
and it flings it
into the East River.

00:05:03.535 --> 00:05:05.271
So I have to figure out
what to do. I run inside.

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:06.939
This guy comes out with a broom,

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:10.609
and he's like, "This has never
happened in all my years."

00:05:10.609 --> 00:05:12.945
Take this sopping-wet bag,
put it in the car,

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:14.947
figure out how to get it
dry cleaned.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:18.117
She walked on stage the next
morning to give a speech,

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:19.952
12 hours later, she had no idea

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.954
her clothes were floating
in the East River.

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.957
-Wow, that's amazing.
-And I didn't tell --

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:27.293
I didn't tell her that story
for a long time.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:28.460
It was like five years.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.229
-I mean, that --
what an advertisement

00:05:30.229 --> 00:05:32.397
for any dry cleaner
that can get --

00:05:32.397 --> 00:05:36.068
if you can get the East River
out of a dress.

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:37.937
-They did well, they did well.
-They did well.

00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:41.774
You obviously, over the years,
gave Hillary a lot of feedback

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:43.442
on things like speeches.
-Yeah.

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:45.778
-Did she have any feedback
on this?

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.112
Did you present it to her?

00:05:47.112 --> 00:05:48.948
Did you look to her for guidance
in any way?

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:51.517
-She was one of the first people
to read the book.

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:54.520
And she gave me -- her notes
were hysterical.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.355
Because she sent me back
the manuscript,

00:05:56.355 --> 00:06:00.860
and in the margins she would
write, "This is very funny."

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.595
Like when I tell the story
of her breaking her elbow

00:06:03.595 --> 00:06:06.432
and me fainting in the hospital
and peeing in my pants.

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:08.133
And she's -- you know, like --

00:06:08.133 --> 00:06:10.370
she's reliving the moments
with me.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.038
But she was just really
encouraging.

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.705
Gave me -- she didn't tell me
to cut anything.

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:15.107
Said put whatever was in there.

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:17.376
In fact, I just talked to her
before I came on the show,

00:06:17.376 --> 00:06:19.478
and I said, "I'm going
to see Seth,

00:06:19.478 --> 00:06:21.814
do you want me to say anything
about you to him?"

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.482
She's like, "Oh, just tell him
you love me."

00:06:23.482 --> 00:06:25.150
I'm like, "Okay!"

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:26.652
I love my boss, Seth.

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:27.986
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.986 --> 00:06:29.655
-Hey, congratulations
on the book.

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:30.823
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:32.325
You deserve to be out here.

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:34.292
Get out of the green room, Huma.
-Thanks, Seth.

00:06:34.292 --> 00:06:36.395
Thrilled to be on the show.
-Huma Abedin, everyone.

00:06:36.395 --> 00:06:38.564
"Both/And: A Life in Many
Worlds" is available now.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:39.565
Be right back.

